AB  - Positive Position Feedback (PPF) is a widely used algorithm for active vibration control of thin-walled structures. Its control performance critically depends on the accurate identification of the participation matrices, which characterize the dynamic electromechanical coupling between piezoelectric actuators/sensors and the host structure. However, this identification remains challenging for multi-input multi-output systems involving composite materials. This study presents a novel data-driven participation matrix identification method for PPF controller design. By constructing a hybrid state vector that incorporates both displacement and strain measurements, we develop a data-driven modelling method called scaled-derivative Dynamic Mode Decomposition with Control (sd-DMDc). The resulting discrete state-space model can accurately reconstruct responses, extract modal parameters, and more importantly, identify participation matrices. Based on this model, we formulate an sd-DMDc-based PPF (sd-DMDc-PPF) controller and validate its effectiveness experimentally on a thin-walled composite plate with a non-collocated Macro Fiber Composite (MFC) configuration. Experimental results demonstrate effective suppression of six vibration modes using only two MFC actuators and two MFC sensors. A comparative study confirms that the sd-DMDc-PPF controller achieves superior performance over the mainstream Least Squares Estimation (LSE)-based PPF controller. An acoustic excitation experiment further validates the capability and robustness of the proposed sd-DMDc-PPF controller under operating conditions. By providing more accurate participation matrices, the sd-DMDc model improves the PPF control performance and highlights the potential advantages of the data-driven modelling paradigm for such applications.
ER  - 

