AB  - A deep learning-based microstructure characterization and reconstruction framework is developed to generate an ensemble of stochastic representative volume elements (SRVEs) that captures intrinsic microstructural variability of unidirectional fiber tows. The workflow integrates quantitative characterization from scanning electron microscopy images, including categorical segmentation and uncertainty-aware descriptors, with a novel conditional discrete diffusion model. The descriptors include the volume fractions of constituents and manufacturing-induced defects, as well as probabilistic measures of their spatial distributions. These descriptors are processed through a multilayer perceptron to generate conditional embedding, which is enforced through multi-head cross-attention blocks within a U-Net. The descriptor-based embedding guides the diffusion model toward a subset of the microstructural space consistent with the observed variability in fiber and defect morphology and volume fractions, as well as in fiber-related spatial distributions and higher-order point statistics. Incorporating descriptors accelerates training convergence and more accurately captures microstructural variability relative to descriptor-guidance-free diffusion models. To streamline micromechanical analysis, geometric periodicity is imposed during diffusion model sampling by applying circular padding after each Markov denoising step. A second-order semi-analytical micromechanical method is then used to evaluate the effective composite properties of the SRVE ensemble, enabling propagation of stochasticity to the weave scale through bottom-up hierarchical multiscale modeling.
