AB  - An advanced multi contact-point detection method for the identification of all bridge modal parameters using a moving two-axle test vehicle is proposed by modifying the stochastic subspace identification (SSI) procedure. Initially, closed-form solutions are derived for both the bridge and vehicle-bridge contact point responses, along with a back-calculation formula to extract the front/rear contact responses from vehicle data. The system’s discrete state-space equations are then established for the two contact responses, from which modal frequencies and damping ratios are obtained by eigenvalue analysis. To mitigate damping-induced mode shape distortion, a segmentation approach making use of the two contact points is introduced, allowing the recovery of mode shapes via the spatial correlation of SSI-derived modal amplitudes. Theoretical, numerical, and experimental studies confirm that: (1) Multi contact-point responses can be effectively dealt with by using a modified SSI method; (2) the front and rear contacts provide the spatial relation needed for bridge identification, independent of vehicle frequencies; (3) segmentation enables mode shapes to be derived without damping distortion; (4) the technique was validated by field tests for bridge frequencies and mode shapes; and (5) it applies to beam-like structures with rough pavement, aided by ongoing traffic.
