AB  - In this paper, a satellite attitude linearization and LQR control strategy, based on the Deep Koopman operator, is presented to address the reliance on linearization assumptions found in traditional attitude control methods when complex nonlinear dynamics are encountered. By introducing the powerful nonlinear modeling capability of deep neural networks, the complex characteristics of the satellite attitude dynamics system are accurately described, and a high-dimensional linearized model can be obtained without relying on an accurate mathematical model, which can only be trained using historical data. This method not only overcomes the traditional Koopman algorithm’s dependence on known physical models or differential equations but also demonstrates better real-time adaptability and generalization ability. Specifically, the Eulerian method is first applied to discretize the satellite attitude-rotation dynamics equations and construct a nonlinear discrete state-space model. Then, the state upscaling is realized based on a deep autoencoder, and the high-dimensional linear dynamic system parameters are designed to capture the behavioral characteristics of the nonlinear system. To restore the physical significance of the high-dimensional state vectors, a decoder is further developed for dimensionality reduction processing, which recovers the low-dimensional state vectors. In addition, we define a comprehensive loss function including autoencoder loss, prediction loss, metric loss, and controllability loss to optimize the learning process of the whole framework. Experimental results show that the proposed LQR controller based on the Deep Koopman operator outperforms other nonlinear control methods (e.g., NMPC and iLQR) in terms of control accuracy and energy loss. Especially for the presence of Gaussian random disturbances, the controller is still able to maintain small tracking errors and low energy consumption, proving its robustness and adaptability. Therefore, this study provides a new theoretical foundation and technical framework for future satellite attitude control, especially in missions requiring fast response and high accuracy, showing significant advantages. Lastly, simulations and comparisons are given to demonstrate the effectiveness and excellent tracking performance of the proposed control scheme.
