AB  - To address the insufficient sintering resistance of TBC topcoats for high-efficiency gas turbines, a discrete diffusion barrier was proposed. A novel anti-sintering (NAS) topcoat was prepared from core-shell YSZ@LZ powder (YSZ core partly covered by LZ shell), with discrete LZ lamellae as sintering diffusion barriers. Microstructural evolution, sintering behavior, and cracking were analyzed after exposure at 1400 °C. The results showed that spherical YSZ@LZ core-shell powder could be successfully produced by dual-feed atmospheric plasma spraying. The NAS coating deposited from YSZ@LZ powder consisted of a YSZ matrix with uniformly dispersed LZ lamellae. Free-standing coatings were then exposed isothermally at 1400 °C for up to 50 h to track the local evolution of intersplat gaps. After 50 h of isothermal exposure at 1400 °C, the measured intersplat-gap area reduction was 12.26% at YSZ/YSZ interfaces and 8.89% at YSZ/LZ heterogeneous interfaces; thus, gap closure at the YSZ/LZ interface was lower by 3.37 percentage points, corresponding to a 27.5% relative decrease. This result indicates local inhibition of intersplat-gap healing at heterogeneous interfaces. The measured hardness, elastic modulus, and indentation fracture toughness after thermal exposure were comparable between the NAS and YSZ coatings.
