AB  - In this paper we explore Discrete Diffusion Monte Carlo (DDMC) and Implicit Monte Carlo (IMC) in thermal radiative transfer in the difference formulation. The difference formulation is a source manipulation variance reduction technique, which is advantageous when the problem contains regions that are near equilibrium. Traditionally, the numerical instability will occur in regions of the problem with sharp spatial gradients in the IMC or DDMC simulation in the difference formulation. We find the selection of reference fields is very important to the numerical results. After analysis, we find that the smaller the energy emitted by the sources in each time step, the smaller the statistical noise of the calculation results. In the difference formulation, when the reference field is selected differently, the total energy due to the sources during each time step is also different. Thus, we propose the concept of the global optimal reference field in which the energy emitted by the sources is minimized in each time step. Although it is difficult to find the global optimal reference field directly, we introduce a scheme to select a relatively good reference field. Our method employs a hybrid transport diffusion method to solve the transport equation in the difference formulation. The IMC equations and the DDMC equations are derived from the transformed equations. In addition, we propose a method to deal with the interface between optically thick and optically thin regions in the difference formulation. Numerical tests show that our method is accurate and can significantly reduce the statistical noise, even in regions of the problem with sharp spatial gradients.
