AB  - This article investigates the pedestrian group as an emergent agent. The article explores empirical data and provides preliminary evidence to support the formulation of pedestrian-group walking-behavior as a hybrid-system; with focus on emergent agency state space, formation state space, and other recurring patterns of walking behavior. In this analysis, pedestrian trajectories extracted from surveillance videos are used along with previously published manually annotated pedestrian group memberships. We conducted additional manual expert evaluation of observed groups, produced new manual annotations for relevant events pertaining to group behavior, and extracted metrics relevant to group formation. Mixed qualitative and quantitative analysis is used to develop a hypothesis for discrete state spaces for the group modeled as a hybrid system. Eventually, with more evidence to justify the proposed model, the model could help capture detailed and structured expectations around group behavior throughout its life-cycle; from pedestrian walking independently to the emergence of a collective intention where group members tend to maintain bounded distance between each other. The presented analysis suggests that disturbances to this bounded distance often occur in association with changes in either their agency or their formation states. We summarized the observed group behavior along with the sequences of state transitions into abstract patterns, which can aid in the development of more detailed group agents in simulation and in the design of engineering systems to interact with such groups.
