AB  - Hybrid meshes for computational flow simulations are of importance due to the complexity of both the geometry and the flow fields and the need for automation. Meshes can consist of a mix of hexahedra, prisms, and tetrahedra, with pyramids serving as transitional elements between hexahedra or prisms and tetrahedra. The local regions where elements of different types meet constitute the mesh interfaces. These are a significant source of error in flow simulations. A large number of different interface configurations are typically encountered, which have distinct characteristics in terms of the local accuracy of spatial discretizations. In the present work, optimal construction of a spectrum of interfaces is performed based on a priori error analysis. The examined interface configurations are typically encountered in hybrid meshes used for boundary layer flow simulations. Numerical error estimators are derived directly from the truncation error corresponding to the discretization of first-order spatial derivatives. The error estimators are independent of the mesh size and primarily characterize the shape and topology of the interface configurations. Techniques for optimal placement of interface vertices are proposed, resulting in significant error reduction. The optimal vertex placement can be applied either during mesh generation or subsequently as an adaptation strategy. Appropriate equations are formulated to estimate the optimal location of the vertices. Comparison of the error is made between different types of interfaces with both vertex-centered and cell-centered finite volume discretizations.
