AB  - Understanding the random walks of molecules on the surface is essential for bridging the gap between idealized models and real material systems. In this study, random walk movement of C60 fullerene molecule is investigated on a quasi-tetragonal-phase fullerene (qTPC60) monolayer using molecular dynamics simulations. The qTPC60 substrate provides periodic adsorption sites, where C60 exhibits thermally activated random walks on these tetrahedral sites. Potential energy analysis reveals energy barrier of 0.4 eV for lateral transitions (up, down, left, right) of C60 on the surface. According to Transition state theory, difference of free energies between two adjacent sites is 0.0017 eV, which shows the similar tendency of molecules to each neighboring adsorption site. This theory predicts the rate constant of transitions as 0.23ns−1, which leads to mean dwell time of 4.35 ns the molecule experiences on an adsorption site. At temperatures between 450 and 550 K, the C60 molecule undergoes a two-dimensional discrete random walk, with step-lengths of 8.4 Å. In agreement with random walk theory, the probability of finding C60 at a given position follows a gaussian distribution function, with standard deviations of σx=11.56Å and σy=9.53Å, in x- and y-axis directions. The mean square displacement shows a linear relation with time which confirms the normal diffusion regime, with diffusion coefficients of 8.92 and 13.76 Å2/ns in x- and y-axis directions at 500 K. The findings reveal that C60 exhibits random walk dynamics on qTPC60, establishing this system as an ideal model for studying molecular transport in nanostructured materials.
