AB  - We propose a super-resolution (SR) framework that embeds a learnable fractional p(x, t)-diffusion equation as a differentiable regularization block inside a convolutional neural network. The spatially varying exponent p(x, t) adapts the diffusion locally: p ≈ 1 at edges activates total-variation-like smoothing that preserves discontinuities, while p ≈ 2 in homogeneous regions applies stronger noise suppression. We prove that each discretized diffusion step is a strict contraction in ℓ2, providing an explicit Lipschitz bound for the full network and, consequently, a certified worst-case reconstruction guarantee under bounded input perturbations. The model is trained via an adversarial min–max objective that jointly optimizes clean-image fidelity and robustness against ℓ∞-bounded attacks. Experiments on five standard benchmarks (Set5, Set14, BSD100, Urban100, Manga109) under clean, impulse-noise, and adversarial conditions show that the proposed method improves PSNR over state-of-the-art SR networks while maintaining competitive performance on clean data.
